  title={"The Reality Code Engine: One Lossless Form for Native, Verified, Low-Energy Programming"}
  description={"About: The Reality Code Engine: One Lossless Form for Native, Verified, Low-Energy Programming. This paper describes the Reality Code Engine (RCE), a system in which a program is written once as a state machine, called a Form, and code in other languages is generated from it."}
>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research paper · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      The Reality Code Engine: One Lossless Form for Native, Verified, Low-Energy Programming
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      This paper describes the Reality Code Engine (RCE), a system in which a program is written once as a state machine, called a Form, and code in other languages is generated from it. It presents the written specification of the language, the reference checker that implements it and emits Rust, Python, JavaScript and Scala (the Scala output does not yet agree with the reference), the Lean proofs of its theorems, registered measurements of the dimension of its programs, the design of the engine that finds, checks and reuses solutions, and the design by which every language is to be lifted into the Form and emitted from it. It states how the claims could be shown false and the protocols of the measurements still to be made.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>A program is written once, in one precise form, and the computer produces the same program from it in Rust, Python and JavaScript today. The design is for every language, and most of it is not built yet.</li>
        <li>Everything a program can do is declared in its form, and the language requires every input to have a declared outcome, a step or a recorded refusal, rather than an undefined one that is found and fixed later.</li>
        <li>In the design of the engine, a fix that passes the checks is saved and reused, so that the same problem does not cost time or energy twice.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>


    <figure class="mt-10 glass-card p-4 fig-wide">
      <a class="fig-zoom" href="/figures/rce/spec/rce-overview.svg" aria-label="Enlarge figure"><picture><source type="image/svg+xml" srcset="/figures/rce/spec/rce-overview.svg" /><img src="/figures/rce/spec/rce-overview.png" alt="RCE overview: source, checker, Form P = (S, E, delta, D, lambda), hash-chained log D, emit targets Rust, Python, JS and Scala, host and compute fabrics" width="796" height="436" class="fig-img w-full rounded bg-white" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></picture></a>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">RCE at a glance. The checker accepts a program or names the rule it breaks; an accepted program has a Form; every step is appended to the log D; the emitters translate the checked program, not the Form, to Rust, Python and JavaScript, which each pass 1,459 conformance tests with 0 failures, and to Scala, which does not yet agree with the reference; a host runs it. Two parts of the figure are design: the emit targets drawn out of the Form, since lifting and emitting every language through the Form is the design goal of the proposal <a href="https://github.com/openIE-dev/rce/blob/main/spec/proposals/rce-form.md">spec/proposals/rce-form.md</a>, and the row of compute fabrics, since the reference executor runs on CPUs only (<a href="/papers/rce/#125-the-library-and-the-compute-fabric">RCE paper, §12.5</a>). <span class="fig-links"><a href="/figures/rce/spec/rce-overview.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full size</a> · <a href="/figures/rce/spec/rce-overview.png">PNG</a></span>. More figures: <a href="https://github.com/openIE-dev/rce/tree/main/docs/figures">openIE-dev/rce docs/figures</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

